le don't change. They just get better at hiding.' But Kate Sullivan had changed — hadn't she? The question followed her through the farmers market, past the wildflowers, into her dreams."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Choice', 'text': "He laughed through tears, the same laugh, and the sunset did its thing, and Maple Hollow held its breath again. This time nobody interrupted. This time they stayed.\n\nShe found him at the shore, where it started. 'I choose,' she said, breathless, 'the thing that scares me. I choose you. I've always chosen you — I just took seventeen years to say it.'\n\nher grandmother Rose cried at the news and denied it. The whole of Maple Hollow seemed to conspire in their happiness: the bookshop stayed open late, the bakery made a cake, the wildflowers bloomed on schedule."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise', 'text': "The wedding was in Maple Hollow, in the season of wildflowers. Her grandmother Rose's quilt was on the bed. her grandmother Rose gave a speech that made everyone cry laughing.\n\nthe heart knows first — Kate Sullivan wrote it in the new journal, the one for the next seventeen years. In Maple Hollow, under the stars, with love: the whole story, and only just beginning.\n\nHe proposed with a plane ticket — fitting, her grandmother Rose said, since it started everything. Kate Sullivan said yes before he finished asking, because seventeen years is long enough to wait for a sentence."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue in Wildflowers', 'text': "Kate Sullivan walks the shore most evenings, sometimes alone, sometimes with him, always with Maple Hollow around her like a held breath. The sunsets haven't changed. She has — into someone who stays.\n\nYears later, a plane ticket sits on the mantel still, a little faded, entirely cherished. The house is full of noise: children, dogs, her grandmother Rose's children, the whole beautiful chaos of a life chosen.\n\nAnd when the children ask about a plane ticket, Kate Sullivan tells them: it was a map. Not to a place — to a person. To the bravest thing she ever did, which was to come home."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

